       <h2 id="s3"><span class="section-number">3</span>Level, slope and curvature</h2>
 
-      <p>For nonzero vectors \(u, v \in \R^n\), the <em>cosine</em> of the angle between them is \(\cos(u, v) = u^{\top}v / (\|u\|\,\|v\|)\). Let \(\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n\) be the tenors and \(\bar\tau\) their mean. Write \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\) for the constant vector in \(\R^n\) whose \(n\) entries all equal \(1\), and \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_T\) for the analogous vector in \(\R^T\). Three shapes over the tenors are used below: \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\); the centred tenors \(\tau - \bar\tau\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\); and the centred squared tenors, orthogonalized against the first two. Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, orthogonalizing means subtracting from a shape its orthogonal projection onto the span of the earlier shapes (Lesson 7, Theorem 1.2). Assume that the tenors take at least three distinct values, which forces \(n \ge 3\). With exactly two distinct values, \(\tau^{2} = -ab\,\boldsymbol{\iota}_n + (a + b)\tau\) on the tenors, where \(a\) and \(b\) are the two values, so the centred squared tenors lie in the span of the first two shapes and the curvature shape is zero. With one distinct value the slope shape is zero as well. Under the three-value assumption the three shapes are nonzero and mutually orthogonal, and the earlier shapes are independent, so Lesson 7, Theorem 1.2 applies at each orthogonalization step.</p>
+      <p>For nonzero vectors \(u, v \in \R^n\), the <em>cosine</em> of the angle between them is \(\cos(u, v) = u^{\top}v / (\|u\|\,\|v\|)\). Let \(\tau_1, \ldots, \tau_n\) be the tenors and \(\bar\tau\) their mean. Write \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\) for the constant vector in \(\R^n\) whose \(n\) entries all equal \(1\), and \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_T\) for the analogous vector in \(\R^T\). Three shapes over the tenors are used below: \(\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\); the centred tenors \(\tau - \bar\tau\boldsymbol{\iota}_n\); and the centred squared tenors, orthogonalized against the first two. Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, orthogonalizing means subtracting from a shape its orthogonal projection onto the span of the earlier shapes (Lesson 7, Theorem 1.2). Assume that the tenors take at least three distinct values, which forces \(n \ge 3\). With exactly two distinct values, \(\tau^{2} = -ab\,\boldsymbol{\iota}_n + (a + b)\tau\) on the tenors, where \(a\) and \(b\) are the two values, so the centred squared tenors lie in the span of the first two shapes and the curvature shape is zero. With one distinct value the slope shape is zero as well. Under the three-value assumption the three shapes are nonzero and mutually orthogonal, and the earlier shapes are independent, so Lesson 7, Theorem 1.2 applies at each orthogonalization step. The curvature shape is nonzero because a relation \(\tau_j^{2} = a + b\,\tau_j\) at every tenor would make the quadratic \(x^{2} - bx - a\), which has leading coefficient \(1\), vanish at three distinct points, and a nonzero quadratic has at most two roots.</p>
 
       <div class="block definition">
         <span class="block-title">Definition 3.1 <span class="name">(level, slope and curvature shapes)</span></span>
-        <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4 and that the tenors take at least three distinct values (Section 3), let the three shapes above be normalized to norm \(1\), giving \(f_1\) (level), \(f_2\) (slope) and \(f_3\) (curvature), which are orthonormal by construction. Let \(v_1, \ldots, v_n\) be the principal directions of Definition 1.3. The <em>match</em> of \(f_j\) is the principal direction \(v_k\) with the largest \(|\cos(v_k, f_j)|\) over \(k = 1, \ldots, n\), provided exactly one principal direction attains that largest value. If two or more principal directions attain it, \(f_j\) has no match and receives no label. A principal direction that is the match of \(f_1\), \(f_2\) or \(f_3\) is called <dfn id="def-level-slope-curvature" data-concept="level-slope-curvature">level-like</dfn>, <em>slope-like</em> or <em>curvature-like</em>, respectively. These are labels for shapes, and they define no yield-curve factor. Lab 1 computes the matches on a SIMULATED panel, and they make no claim about the shape of any observed yield curve.</p>
+        <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4 and that the tenors take at least three distinct values (Section 3), let the three shapes above be normalized to norm \(1\), giving \(f_1\) (level), \(f_2\) (slope) and \(f_3\) (curvature), which are orthonormal by construction. Let \(v_1, \ldots, v_n\) be the principal directions of Definition 1.3. The <em>match</em> of \(f_j\) is the principal direction \(v_k\) with the largest \(|\cos(v_k, f_j)|\) over \(k = 1, \ldots, n\), provided exactly one principal direction attains that largest value. If two or more principal directions attain it, \(f_j\) has no match and receives no label. A principal direction that is the match of \(f_1\), \(f_2\) or \(f_3\) is called <em>level-like</em>, <em>slope-like</em> or <em>curvature-like</em>, respectively. These labels, applied to principal directions, are the <dfn id="def-level-slope-curvature" data-concept="level-slope-curvature">level-like, slope-like and curvature-like principal directions</dfn>. They are labels for shapes, and they define no yield-curve factor. Lab 1 computes the matches on a SIMULATED panel, and they make no claim about the shape of any observed yield curve.</p>
