-            <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, let \(\tilde X = FB^{\top}\), where \(F\) is \(T \times 3\) with centred columns and \(B\) is \(n \times 3\) with orthonormal columns. Prove that \(M = BGB^{\top}\) with \(G = F^{\top}F/(T - 1)\). If \(G = Q\Gamma Q^{\top}\) is a spectral decomposition, show that the nonzero eigenvalues of \(M\) are the nonzero diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\), with eigenvectors \(BQ\). Show that \(M\) has exactly three nonzero eigenvalues when \(F\) has rank \(3\).</p>
+            <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, let \(\tilde X = HB^{\top}\), where \(H\) is \(T \times 3\) with centred columns and \(B\) is \(n \times 3\) with orthonormal columns. Prove that \(M = BGB^{\top}\) with \(G = H^{\top}H/(T - 1)\). If \(G = Q\Gamma Q^{\top}\) is a spectral decomposition, show that the nonzero eigenvalues of \(M\) are the nonzero diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\), with eigenvectors \(BQ\). Show that \(M\) has exactly three nonzero eigenvalues when \(H\) has rank \(3\).</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>Substituting, \(M = \frac{1}{T-1}(FB^{\top})^{\top}(FB^{\top}) = \frac{1}{T-1}BF^{\top}FB^{\top} = BGB^{\top}\). Let \(W = BQ\). Then \(W^{\top}W = Q^{\top}B^{\top}BQ = Q^{\top}Q = I\), since \(B^{\top}B = I\), and \(MW = BGB^{\top}BQ = BGQ = BQ\Gamma = W\Gamma\). So each column of \(W\) is an eigenvector of \(M\), with the eigenvalue in the matching diagonal entry of \(\Gamma\). The columns of \(W\) span the column space of \(B\), because \(Q\) is invertible. If \(x\) is orthogonal to that column space, then \(B^{\top}x = 0\), so \(Mx = BGB^{\top}x = 0\). An orthonormal basis of that complement, taken as eigenvectors with eigenvalue \(0\), completes \(W\) to an orthonormal eigenbasis of \(M\). Hence the nonzero eigenvalues of \(M\) are exactly the nonzero diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\). If \(F\) has rank \(3\), then \(G\) is invertible, so all three diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\) are nonzero, and \(M\) has exactly three nonzero eigenvalues, those of \(G\).</p>
+              <p>Substituting, \(M = \frac{1}{T-1}(HB^{\top})^{\top}(HB^{\top}) = \frac{1}{T-1}BH^{\top}HB^{\top} = BGB^{\top}\). Let \(W = BQ\). Then \(W^{\top}W = Q^{\top}B^{\top}BQ = Q^{\top}Q = I\), since \(B^{\top}B = I\), and \(MW = BGB^{\top}BQ = BGQ = BQ\Gamma = W\Gamma\). So each column of \(W\) is an eigenvector of \(M\), with the eigenvalue in the matching diagonal entry of \(\Gamma\). The columns of \(W\) span the column space of \(B\), because \(Q\) is invertible. If \(x\) is orthogonal to that column space, then \(B^{\top}x = 0\), so \(Mx = BGB^{\top}x = 0\). An orthonormal basis of that complement, taken as eigenvectors with eigenvalue \(0\), completes \(W\) to an orthonormal eigenbasis of \(M\). Hence the nonzero eigenvalues of \(M\) are exactly the nonzero diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\). If \(H\) has rank \(3\), then \(G\) is invertible, so all three diagonal entries of \(\Gamma\) are nonzero, and \(M\) has exactly three nonzero eigenvalues, those of \(G\).</p>
             </details>
